  {
   "id": "P12",
   "type": "proposition",
   "name": null,
   "statement": "Given an $\\angle~BAC$, there must exist a point $F$, s.t., $F \\neq A$ and $\\angle~BAF = \\angle~CAF$.",
   "origin": "paper",
   "tags": [
    "leanok",
    "lean"
   ],
   "section": "13.1 Commentary",
   "label": "proposition_9",
   "lean_names": [
    "Elements.Book1.proposition_9"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "PR_P12",
   "type": "proof",
   "name": null,
   "statement": "Euclid's proof has two problems. First, when constructing $F$, it fails to state the requirement that $F$ and $A$ must be on different sides of $DE$. Second, Euclid did not rule out the possibility that $F$ lies on $AB$ or $AC$. If that could happen, $\\triangle~DAF$ or $\\triangle~EAF$ wouldn't have existed, and the original proof would have failed. To prove $F$ is not on $AB$, let's first assume $F$ is on $AB$ (Fig.~\\ref{fig:prop_9}). Apply Prop.~\\ref{proposition_5} to $\\triangle~ADE$ to derive $\\angle~FDE = \\angle~CED$. Apply Prop.~\\ref{proposition_5'} to $\\triangle~FDE$ to derive $\\angle~FDE = \\angle~FED$. Note that $\\angle~CED~>~\\angle~FED$. Contradiction. Similarly, we can prove $F$ is not on $AC$.",
   "origin": "paper",
   "tags": [
    "leanok"
   ],
   "section": "13.1 Commentary",
   "label": null,
   "proves": [
    "P12"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "P13",
   "type": "proposition",
   "name": null,
   "statement": "Given an $\\angle~BAC$, there must exist a point $F$, s.t., $F \\neq A$, $\\angle~BAF = \\angle~CAF$; $F$, $C$ are on the same side of $AB$; and $F$, $B$ are on the same side of $AC$.",
   "origin": "paper",
   "tags": [
    "leanok",
    "lean"
   ],
   "section": "13.1 Commentary",
   "label": "proposition_9'",
   "lean_names": [
    "Elements.Book1.proposition_9'"
   ]
